    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d214">

                <pb facs="0410" n="306" sameAs="frus:frus1890/pg306"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Phelps to Mr.
                            Blaine.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1890-03-01"><hi rendition="#italic">March</hi> 1, 1890</date>.
                        (Received March 17.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No 79.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: It occurred to rue that it might be useful
                    to have in the files of the Department which preserve the papers connected with
                    Samoa, on a single sheet and in print, the extracts from the three great organs
                    of German political sentiment and thought which I have already forwarded
                    separately. They indicate the absolute unanimity—so far as newspapers reflect
                    it—of German public sentiment with reference to the Samoan treaty. I take the
                    liberty of inclosing some twenty copies of these extracts in print.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Wm. Walter
                    Phelps.</hi></signed></closer>



                <xi:include href="d214-a1.xml" xpointer="d214-a1"/>
            </div>
